{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And I will turn to you.</b> That My will shall cleave to you. Know that this is the ultimate goal of the intellect (sekhel), for the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, is the life of the World to Come, as it is written (Psalms 30:6): ״כי רגע באפו חיים ברצונו״. And this too is among the allusions of the Torah, in which there are matters that are a promise for the future; therefore you will find in this parashah both corporeal and intellectual promises, for what He said, ״והתהלכתי בתוככם״ — 'and I will walk among you' — is not to be understood as a bodily promise but as a promise for the soul in the World to Come, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 3:8): ״מתהלך בגן״. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded this verse, ״והתהלכתי בתוככם״ — they said: the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to stroll with the righteous in the Garden of Eden, and His glory is among them. He compared the attainment of the delight that belongs to the souls to a מחול (dance-circle), which is round, for the round has no beginning and no end, and so too that delight has no end and no limit; and since the circle always surrounds the point and the point is in the middle, therefore it says ״וכבודו ביניהם״ — 'and His glory is among them.' And so He mentioned here ״בתוככם״ — 'among you' — because He likened Israel to a dance-circle and Himself to the point. And after He said ״והתהלכתי בתוככם״ He said ״והייתי לכם לאלהים״ — 'and I will be to you for Elohim' — and this is what they said: each and every one will point to Him with his finger, as it is said (Isaiah 25:9): ״הנה אלהינו זה״; and this is a parable for the nearness of knowledge and attainment, like one who has acquaintance with his fellow and identifies him and recognizes him with clarity; and this expression is not to be understood literally as a thing standing before the person, but it is in the manner of what is said (Exodus 32:1): ״כי זה משה האיש״ — 'for this Moses, the man' — for they already had acquaintance with him though he was not standing among them. And it has been explained here that the reward of the World of Souls is so great that the verse interspersed it among the corporeal promises, relying on the intellect of the discerning person to understand it from within them; and because of the surpassing greatness of that world, the Torah never mentioned its matter explicitly anywhere, for it is hidden and deep beyond the grasp of individuals while they are still in matter; and even the prophets did not prophesy of it; and so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: all the prophets, all of them, prophesied only for the days of the Messiah, but for the World to Come, ״עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך״ — they explained thereby its surpassing depth and hiddenness, that even the prophets did not prophesy of it. And so this entire parashah, whether in corporeal promises or in intellectual promises, is a future promise, for it was never fulfilled but will be fulfilled in the time of perfection."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The phrase עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך is a paraphrase of Isaiah 64:3 (קרא in some editions); he cites it as a known rabbinic dictum (Berakhot 34b)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:48.294713120Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}